<ArticleLayout slug="china-or-india-for-creative-production" ctaLabel="Talk to the studio" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Search this question and you get two kinds of page. Software-outsourcing comparisons written fifteen to twenty years ago, and offshore creative production articles that treat India as the default and never evaluate China at all. Neither compares the two regions on what a production buyer actually decides on.</p>

  <p>This page does, from government statistics and named instruments, and it starts with where India wins. hubStudio operates studios in China and in the Philippines, so it has a side. Conceding first is the only way the rest of it is worth reading.</p>

  <h2>Is China or India better for creative production?</h2>

  <p>Neither, as a general answer. The question resolves on four specifics: whether your work is English-first, whether it has to reach a European working day, whether the product physically exists near where it is made, and whether it runs on Chinese platforms.</p>

  <p>India wins the first two on the evidence below. China wins the second two, and the margin on category proximity is not close. If your answer set splits across both columns, that is a real result and worth knowing before anyone quotes.</p>

  <h2>Where does India win on creative outsourcing?</h2>

  <p>Five places, and they are the reasons India became the default.</p>

  <p><strong>English, by a measurable margin.</strong></p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">India scores 484 and ranks 74th; China scores 464 and ranks 86th. Both sit in the index's low band and below the 488 global average.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the 2025 edition of a published English proficiency index covering 123 countries and regions, scored from the results of 2.2 million adults who took the publisher's free online English test, a self-selected sample.</p>

  <p>Twelve places apart, on a self-selected sample of people who chose to take a free online English test. That is a real gap and a weak instrument, and both things are true at once. Note also where both countries land: the low band. The honest version of India's advantage is that it is better, not that it is good by the index's own standard.</p>

  <p><strong>The European working day, which is arithmetic rather than opinion.</strong></p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">India Standard Time runs at UTC+5:30 and China Standard Time at UTC+8, one zone for the whole country. A nine-to-six day in India overlaps a nine-to-six London day by about 3.5 hours in winter and 4.5 in summer. The same day in China overlaps it by about one hour in winter and two in summer. Neither overlaps a nine-to-six New York day at all.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published civil time offsets, arithmetic shown so the reader can check it, September 2026.</p>

  <p>Three to four times the daily overlap with London. For work that needs live review rather than handover, that difference decides the week. And the last sentence matters just as much: a US buyer choosing between these two regions is choosing between two overnight relationships, not between one live one and one overnight one.</p>

  <p><strong>A lighter cross-border data regime.</strong> Covered in full further down, because it deserves more than a bullet.</p>

  <p><strong>Incumbency in English-language production.</strong> The SERP itself is the evidence. Every page that ranks for offshore creative production treats India as the starting point. Two decades of that produces working practices, contract templates and account managers who have done it before, and none of that shows up in a statistic.</p>

  <p><strong>A large arts-heavy graduate base.</strong></p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">India had 45.0 million students enrolled in higher education in 2023-24, a gross enrollment ratio of about 30 percent, with 59,533 of 64,756 registered institutions responding. Arts is the largest undergraduate grouping at 32.1 percent.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: India's Ministry of Education, All India Survey on Higher Education 2023-24, released 30 August 2026, a web-based return completed by each institution at over 90 percent participation.</p>

  <p>Arts as the single largest undergraduate grouping is a genuine structural advantage for creative work, and it is published by India's own education ministry rather than inferred.</p>

  <h2>What this comparison cannot honestly do</h2>

  <p>Four things, and every page that pretends otherwise is making them up. Naming them is not throat-clearing. It is most of what separates this page from the ones above it.</p>

  <p><strong>Nobody can size the creative talent pool.</strong></p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Neither release publishes a design or creative-arts graduate count, so the creative talent pool cannot be sized from public statistics on either side.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: both government releases read in full, September 2026.</p>

  <p>Two national education ministries, tens of millions of students each, and no published count of design graduates in either. Any article giving you one has either found a private dataset it is not naming or made it up.</p>

  <p><strong>Nobody can compare the wages.</strong> Both countries publish an earnings figure. The figures are not comparable, and the reason is worth spelling out because the comparison is so tempting.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's average annual wage in culture, sport and entertainment was 126,040 yuan in urban non-private units in 2024, down 1.0 percent nominally, and 61,669 yuan in urban private units, up 3.8 percent. The all-industry averages were 124,110 and 69,476 yuan.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: China's National Bureau of Statistics, 2024 average wages of employed persons in urban units, published 16 May 2025, gross pre-tax wage bill divided by average employment, by industry.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">India's average monthly earnings for regular wage and salaried employees were 20,702 rupees in 2023-24, across all industries with no creative breakout.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: India's Periodic Labour Force Survey annual report for July 2023 to June 2024, a rotational-panel household survey of self-reported earnings. This figure is not comparable with the Chinese one: monthly against annual, all salaried workers against one named industry, household survey against establishment survey.</p>

  <p>Monthly against annual. All salaried workers against one named industry. A household survey of what people say they earn against an establishment survey dividing a wage bill by headcount. Any multiple built from those two numbers is fabricated, however confidently it is printed, and you will see it printed.</p>

  <p><strong>Nobody publishes physical studio capacity.</strong> No sound-stage count, no studio-floor area, for either country. Screen counts and film certification counts exist and are not substitutes for them.</p>

  <p><strong>Nobody publishes manufacturing share by category.</strong> The aggregate exists. The split for electronics, beauty, fashion or appliances does not, in any source this page would cite.</p>

  <p>Those four gaps are why the sections below argue from structure and from instruments rather than from a scoreboard.</p>

  <h2>How big is the talent pool on each side?</h2>

  <p>One thing can be compared here, and it is worth doing carefully because it is the closest either country comes to an answer.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China had 48.46 million people in higher education of all kinds in 2024, a gross enrollment ratio of 60.80 percent, and produced 10.5938 million undergraduate and junior-college graduates that year.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: China's Ministry of Education, 2024 National Education Development Statistical Communique, published 11 June 2025, a compulsory annual return from every approved institution.</p>

  <p>Set that beside India's 45.0 million enrolled at a gross enrollment ratio of about 30 percent, and two different pictures emerge from numbers that look similar.</p>

  <p>The enrollment totals are close, within about 8 percent of each other. The enrollment ratios are not: roughly 61 percent against roughly 30 percent. That ratio is the share of the relevant age cohort in higher education, so China is running at about twice India's participation rate against a population of broadly comparable size. Read the other way, India has far more headroom, since a 30 percent ratio means most of the cohort is not enrolled yet.</p>

  <p>One thing that looks comparable here is not. China's release publishes an annual graduate count, 10.5938 million. India's does not publish an equivalent figure, so the two output flows cannot be set against each other, only the enrollment stocks and the ratios.</p>

  <p>Two cautions before anyone builds a plan on that. The instruments differ: the Chinese figure is a compulsory annual return from every approved institution, the Indian one a web-based survey at over 90 percent participation, which is high but not compulsory. And neither number tells you anything about design graduates specifically, which is the gap named above and the one that actually matters here.</p>

  <p>Where India's release does help is composition. Arts being the largest undergraduate grouping at 32.1 percent is a published fact about the shape of the pipeline, and China's communique publishes no equivalent breakdown that would let the same statement be made or refuted on the other side. So on composition, India has evidence and China has none, which is a point for India however you read it.</p>

  <h2>What is China better at for creative production?</h2>

  <p>Three things, and the first is the one that actually moves budgets.</p>

  <p><strong>Category proximity to where the product is made.</strong></p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">India held 2.8 percent of global manufacturing and China 28.8 percent.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: India's Economic Survey 2024-25, tabled 31 January 2025, a share-of-global-output figure published by the government of the country it flatters less.</p>

  <p>One instrument carrying both numbers, published by India's own government, which is why it is the figure worth citing. Ten times the share, and note that this is aggregate manufacturing, not a category split, because no source publishes the split.</p>

  <p>What that aggregate means operationally is simple. If the product is made in Guangdong or Zhejiang, then the sample, the packaging variant and the color correction against a physical unit are a domestic courier away rather than an international shipment and a customs form. A reshoot that takes two days in one region takes two weeks in the other, and no rate card captures that.</p>

  <p><strong>Production infrastructure at scale.</strong></p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China produced 612 feature films and 873 films of all kinds in 2024, sold 1.01 billion cinema admissions, and ended the year with 90,968 screens after adding 4,658.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the China Film Administration's own annual count, published 2 January 2025.</p>

  <p>India's certification body publishes a count too, and it is important not to misuse it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">India's film certification body certified 17,431 films in 2023-24 and 71,963 over five years. That count includes short films, promos, songs and trailers, so it does not sit against China's 612 features.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: figures given to the Rajya Sabha by India's Ministry of Information and Broadcasting, financial year 2023-24.</p>

  <p>Those two figures are counting different things, and setting 17,431 against 612 would be dishonest arithmetic. What the Chinese numbers do support is narrower and still useful: an industrial base adding nearly five thousand screens in a single year is one with crew depth, equipment rental and post capacity behind it. The screens are a proxy for the industry, not for studio floors, which nobody publishes.</p>

  <p><strong>Native platform fluency, which is location-bound.</strong></p>

  <p>Douyin, RedNote and Tmall are China-domiciled platforms whose seller backends, rule centers and review queues operate in Chinese. The specification that matters is not the one in the English-language help article. It is the one in the seller backend, which changes without an English announcement, and which a team outside the market cannot see.</p>

  <p>This is the part of the comparison that is structural rather than a matter of degree. A team can learn a platform's published specs from anywhere. It cannot watch a review queue reject an asset at eleven at night and adjust the next batch before morning unless it is inside the market and inside the language.</p>

  <p>The practical shape of that gap shows up in three places. Rule changes arrive in the backend before they arrive in any English documentation, and sometimes they never arrive in English at all. Rejection reasons come back as Chinese strings written for sellers, not as error codes written for developers, so interpreting them is a language task before it is a technical one. And the review queues themselves run on Chinese business hours, which means the loop between submission, rejection and resubmission either closes the same day or loses one to the calendar.</p>

  <p>None of that is an argument that the work cannot be done from elsewhere. Plenty of it is, and badly is not the same as impossible. It is an argument that the cost of doing it from elsewhere is paid in schedule rather than in rate, which is why it never appears in the comparison that a buyer is usually shown.</p>

  <h2>What neither region solves</h2>

  <p>Worth saying plainly, because a regional decision gets asked to carry more than it can.</p>

  <p><strong>The US working day.</strong> Neither region overlaps a nine-to-six New York day. Choosing between them on time zones only makes sense for European hours, and for American ones both are overnight relationships that live or die on how good the handover document is.</p>

  <p><strong>Origination versus adaptation.</strong> Neither region is inherently better at making the master asset rather than versioning someone else's. That is a question about the individual studio's capability and equipment, not about the country it sits in, and no national statistic will answer it. Ask it directly in the review instead.</p>

  <p><strong>The coordination overhead.</strong> Both regions add a layer of brief-writing, review scheduling and specification discipline that a local supplier does not require. That cost is real, it does not appear on any rate card, and it is roughly the same on both sides.</p>

  <h2>How do IP and data rules differ?</h2>

  <p>Directly, then, since dodging this is the standard move.</p>

  <p>India has the lighter regime for moving personal data out of the country, and it is not close.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act was enacted in 2023 and its implementing Rules were issued on 14 November 2025, with core obligations phased in over eighteen months. Transfers out of India are permitted unless the central government restricts a named destination, with localization only for categories specified for Significant Data Fiduciaries.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the instruments by name, read through independent published legal and policy analyses, September 2026.</p>

  <p>A negative list. Transfer is permitted by default and restricted by exception.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's Personal Information Protection Law has applied since 1 November 2021. The Provisions on Promoting and Regulating Cross-border Data Flows, effective 22 March 2024, require a regulator security assessment before exporting any important data, any personal information held by a critical information infrastructure operator, non-sensitive personal information on more than one million people in a calendar year, or sensitive personal information on more than ten thousand. A clearance lasts three years.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the instruments by name, read through independent published legal analyses, September 2026.</p>

  <p>Named thresholds with a regulator in front of them. On personal data export, India is the lighter regime, and a buyer whose work involves moving customer data across a border should weigh that seriously.</p>

  <p>Now the part that gets left out. Most creative production does not export personal data at all. Product photography, packaging renders, campaign adaptation and ecommerce imagery generate files about objects, not about people. The thresholds above are counted in people, and a shoot of five hundred skincare units contains none.</p>

  <p>Where it does bite is talent. A shoot with recognizable faces, a creator campaign, a database of user-generated content, a model release archive: those are personal information, and that is the point at which the Chinese thresholds start to matter and the assessment timeline enters your schedule. The honest framing is not "China is hard for data." It is that the answer depends on whether people are in your files, and most product work has nobody in it.</p>

