  <h2>How this page was built</h2>

  <p>Every figure above comes from a government release or a named legal instrument, read in September 2026, with the publishing body and the collection method in the source line. Where one instrument carries both countries' numbers, that instrument was preferred, which is why the manufacturing share comes from India's own Economic Survey rather than from two separate sources.</p>

  <p>Four comparisons were attempted and abandoned because the public data does not support them: design graduate counts, a wage ratio, physical studio capacity, and manufacturing share by category. They are named in the body rather than quietly skipped, because their absence is the most useful thing this page can tell you about every other page on the subject.</p>

  <p>No company in either country is named anywhere here, and no figure is attributed to one. The comparison is regional, which is the only level at which public statistics can support it. Reviewed September 2026.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about offshoring creative work</h2>

  <h3>Is China or India better for creative production?</h3>

  <p>It depends on four things: whether the work is English-first, whether it needs a European working-day overlap, whether the product physically exists near where it is manufactured, and whether it runs on Chinese platforms. India wins the first two on published evidence. China wins the last two, with category proximity the widest margin.</p>

  <h3>Where does India win on creative outsourcing?</h3>

  <p>English proficiency, by 484 to 464 on a 2025 index of 123 countries. Working-day overlap with London, about 3.5 to 4.5 hours against roughly one to two. A lighter cross-border data regime, where transfer is permitted unless a destination is restricted. Two decades of incumbency in English-language production. And arts as its largest undergraduate grouping, at 32.1 percent.</p>

  <h3>What is China better at for creative production?</h3>

  <p>Proximity to manufacturing, at 28.8 percent of global output against 2.8 percent, which makes samples and reshoots domestic rather than international. An industrial production base that added 4,658 cinema screens in 2024. And native fluency on Douyin, RedNote and Tmall, whose backends and review queues operate in Chinese and cannot be worked from outside the market.</p>

  <h3>How do IP and data rules differ?</h3>

  <p>India permits personal data transfer out unless the government restricts a named destination. China requires a regulator security assessment above published thresholds, including one million non-sensitive or ten thousand sensitive records, with clearances lasting three years. Both regimes count in people, so product-only files with nobody in them fall outside the thresholds entirely.</p>

  <h3>Which region suits high-volume ecommerce content?</h3>

  <p>China, when the catalog physically exists and is manufactured in Asia. Sample movement is domestic, a reshoot runs in days rather than weeks, and the platform specifications that actually govern acceptance live in Chinese-language seller backends. India suits high-volume English-language work where nothing has to be photographed against a physical unit.</p>
